# The tally counts the thread. The bands count the screen.
#
# `findCount` is every paintable match in the loaded transcript; a band is
# painted only for the part of it the virtualization window actually built
# (main_pane_system.h render_rich_body, `cull`). So the two are equal only when
# the whole thread fits — and every fixture that asserted them equal was short
# enough that nothing was culled, which is to say the rule was confirmed
# exactly where virtualization is a no-op.
#
# This is the other case. One 57-line reply with folding turned off, in a 760px
# window: "migration" is in 56 paintable lines of it and about two thirds of
# them are on screen at any moment. What must hold is that the TALLY does not
# move when the screen does — a reader wants to know how many matches are in
# the thread, the way every editor's "3 of 47" does — while the bands do.
#
# settings: {"window_width":1100,"window_height":760,"open_tabs":[],"active_tab":"","theme":"dark","fold_long_messages":false}
# env: HANABI_LONGMSG_DEMO=1 HANABI_OPEN=rlong HANABI_FIND_DEMO=migration HANABI_FIND_AUDIT=1

# 55 checklist lines plus the sign-off. The thread opens at its end.
expect_text "1 of 56"

# Twenty of those 56 are above the viewport and are not built, so they are not
# painted. This is the number that used to be asserted equal to the tally.
expect_text "bands 36"

# Home jumps to the top of the thread. A different set of lines is built, so a
# different set of bands is painted...
key HOME
wait_frames 20
expect_text "Step 1 of the migration"
expect_text "bands 31"

# ...and the tally is the number it always was, because it was never a
# statement about the screen.
expect_text "1 of 56"
